I do that a lot.  I like to have several containers on my stash
level, though I tend to name them by category of contents.  I
often end up with "Ascension kit" for items that plan towards
the end game like gold detection scrolls; "Armoury" that have
various magical armor options, artifacts and alternate weapons;
"Tool chest" and "Treasure chest" to contain those items in
case I need them, "Cursed stuff" in case I end up with a lot
of blessed remove curse scrolls; and so on.

> Not compelling enough for me to adopt the
> practice, but it makes sense on its own terms.

Agreed.  Name containers with the levels that have interesting
features of the dungeon.  All player-the-person need remember
is the level the stash is on.  Name contents with discoveries.
Even better name contents of each type with its discovered type.
If your "blessed potion of invisibility named bl invis magenta"
ends up a "magenta potion named bl invis magenta" because of a
mindflyer you can call it and know the item.  Thus
player-the-character loses less without resorting to knowledge
from outside the game.

I don't do that sort of naming but then again mindflayers
tend to be near the top of my list for extinctions.


